1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,811
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:01,991 --> 00:00:04,336
There's an invisible ceiling in American money.

3
00:00:04,516 --> 00:00:07,915
Millions of hardworking families reach a certain

4
00:00:07,915 --> 00:00:09,544
point, a decent income,

5
00:00:09,544 --> 00:00:11,385
a house, a couple of cars,

6
00:00:11,385 --> 00:00:13,085
some retirement savings,

7
00:00:13,085 --> 00:00:15,139
and then they stop moving up.

8
00:00:15,319 --> 00:00:16,473
Year after year,

9
00:00:16,473 --> 00:00:17,700
the income rises,

10
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:19,359
but the wealth doesn't.

11
00:00:19,539 --> 00:00:21,327
I call it the middle class trap.

12
00:00:21,507 --> 00:00:23,248
And today we're going to take it

13
00:00:23,248 --> 00:00:24,445
apart, piece by piece.

14
00:00:24,795 --> 00:00:26,972
Two hundred fifty thousand dollars of net

15
00:00:26,972 --> 00:00:27,291
worth.

16
00:00:27,471 --> 00:00:29,822
A line that many middle class families

17
00:00:29,822 --> 00:00:31,369
hover around for decades,

18
00:00:31,369 --> 00:00:33,164
often with most of it tied up

19
00:00:33,164 --> 00:00:34,030
in their home.

20
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:35,774
Here's what makes it a trap.

21
00:00:35,954 --> 00:00:37,626
Nothing about it feels wrong.

22
00:00:37,806 --> 00:00:39,034
You have a job,

23
00:00:39,034 --> 00:00:41,165
a mortgage, a car payment,

24
00:00:41,165 --> 00:00:42,721
kids in activities,

25
00:00:42,721 --> 00:00:44,524
a vacation every year,

26
00:00:44,524 --> 00:00:46,490
and something in a 401k.

27
00:00:46,670 --> 00:00:48,051
You're doing what you were told.

28
00:00:48,231 --> 00:00:49,752
But when you look closely,

29
00:00:49,752 --> 00:00:51,739
almost all of your money is either

30
00:00:51,739 --> 00:00:53,669
locked in your house or spent the

31
00:00:53,669 --> 00:00:54,721
moment it arrives.

32
00:00:54,901 --> 00:00:56,886
And there's very little left working for

33
00:00:56,886 --> 00:00:57,084
you.

34
00:00:57,434 --> 00:00:58,360
In this video,

35
00:00:58,360 --> 00:01:01,337
fifteen traps that keep middle class families

36
00:01:01,337 --> 00:01:04,049
stuck, with real numbers from the Federal

37
00:01:04,049 --> 00:01:05,769
Reserve and other sources.

38
00:01:05,949 --> 00:01:07,588
And we'll look at it from three

39
00:01:07,588 --> 00:01:07,958
angles.

40
00:01:08,138 --> 00:01:11,276
The numbers: where middle class wealth actually

41
00:01:11,276 --> 00:01:11,609
sits.

42
00:01:11,789 --> 00:01:14,528
The psychology: why smart people stay in

43
00:01:14,528 --> 00:01:15,145
the trap.

44
00:01:15,325 --> 00:01:16,384
And the playbook:

45
00:01:16,384 --> 00:01:19,001
how families break through to five hundred

46
00:01:19,001 --> 00:01:20,247
thousand, a million,

47
00:01:20,247 --> 00:01:20,932
and beyond.

48
00:01:21,282 --> 00:01:23,234
If you've ever wondered why you work

49
00:01:23,234 --> 00:01:25,078
so hard and still feel like you're

50
00:01:25,078 --> 00:01:25,999
running in place,

51
00:01:25,999 --> 00:01:27,192
this video is for you.

52
00:01:27,372 --> 00:01:29,404
And if you're just starting out,

53
00:01:29,404 --> 00:01:31,055
it's a map of the traps to

54
00:01:31,055 --> 00:01:33,595
avoid before they ever close around you.

55
00:01:33,775 --> 00:01:35,261
First, the sources.

56
00:01:35,610 --> 00:01:38,433
Household wealth data comes from the Federal

57
00:01:38,433 --> 00:01:41,319
Reserve's Survey of Consumer Finances and the

58
00:01:41,319 --> 00:01:44,655
Federal Reserve's Distributional Financial Accounts.

59
00:01:44,835 --> 00:01:46,611
Income data comes from the U.

60
00:01:46,791 --> 00:01:47,139
S.

61
00:01:47,319 --> 00:01:48,364
Census Bureau.

62
00:01:48,544 --> 00:01:51,689
Retirement account data comes from Vanguard's How

63
00:01:51,689 --> 00:01:53,037
America Saves report.

64
00:01:53,217 --> 00:01:55,908
Car prices come from Kelley Blue Book,

65
00:01:55,908 --> 00:01:58,457
and child care costs from Child Care

66
00:01:58,457 --> 00:01:59,661
Aware of America.

67
00:01:59,841 --> 00:02:02,847
Growth examples use a seven percent average

68
00:02:02,847 --> 00:02:05,155
yearly return as an illustration,

69
00:02:05,155 --> 00:02:06,133
not a promise.

70
00:02:06,313 --> 00:02:07,530
This is education,

71
00:02:07,530 --> 00:02:08,949
not financial advice.

72
00:02:09,298 --> 00:02:10,134
Angle one.

73
00:02:10,314 --> 00:02:11,057
The numbers.

74
00:02:11,237 --> 00:02:13,619
Let's look at where middle class wealth

75
00:02:13,619 --> 00:02:16,061
actually lives, because once you see it,

76
00:02:16,061 --> 00:02:17,588
the trap becomes obvious.

77
00:02:17,938 --> 00:02:18,298
The U.

78
00:02:18,478 --> 00:02:18,803
S.

79
00:02:18,983 --> 00:02:21,957
Census Bureau reported a median household income

80
00:02:21,957 --> 00:02:24,683
of about eighty three thousand seven hundred

81
00:02:24,683 --> 00:02:26,542
dollars in twenty twenty four.

82
00:02:26,722 --> 00:02:29,069
The Federal Reserve found a median family

83
00:02:29,069 --> 00:02:31,188
net worth of about one hundred ninety

84
00:02:31,188 --> 00:02:33,708
two thousand seven hundred dollars in twenty

85
00:02:33,708 --> 00:02:34,338
twenty two.

86
00:02:34,687 --> 00:02:37,152
So the typical household earns over eighty

87
00:02:37,152 --> 00:02:38,560
thousand dollars a year,

88
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:39,968
and has a net worth of a

89
00:02:39,968 --> 00:02:41,963
little more than two years of that

90
00:02:41,963 --> 00:02:42,373
income.

91
00:02:42,553 --> 00:02:44,696
And for many middle class families,

92
00:02:44,696 --> 00:02:46,778
the largest part of that net worth

93
00:02:46,778 --> 00:02:47,696
is home equity.

94
00:02:47,876 --> 00:02:50,906
The Federal Reserve's data consistently shows that

95
00:02:50,906 --> 00:02:53,149
the primary home is the biggest asset

96
00:02:53,149 --> 00:02:55,148
for families in the middle of the

97
00:02:55,148 --> 00:02:58,481
wealth distribution, while wealthier families hold much

98
00:02:58,481 --> 00:03:00,299
more in stocks and businesses.

99
00:03:00,649 --> 00:03:04,198
The Federal Reserve's Distributional Financial Accounts show

100
00:03:04,198 --> 00:03:06,445
the top ten percent of households hold

101
00:03:06,445 --> 00:03:08,811
about two thirds of all household wealth

102
00:03:08,811 --> 00:03:09,461
in America.

103
00:03:09,641 --> 00:03:11,694
The bottom half holds only a few

104
00:03:11,694 --> 00:03:12,207
percent.

105
00:03:12,557 --> 00:03:14,334
Why does that gap exist?

106
00:03:14,514 --> 00:03:16,606
A big part of the answer is

107
00:03:16,606 --> 00:03:17,381
ownership.

108
00:03:17,561 --> 00:03:20,196
Wealthier households own most of the stocks,

109
00:03:20,196 --> 00:03:22,890
the businesses and the investment properties,

110
00:03:22,890 --> 00:03:25,284
assets that can grow faster than incomes

111
00:03:25,284 --> 00:03:26,362
over long periods.

112
00:03:26,542 --> 00:03:29,022
Middle class households mostly own their home

113
00:03:29,022 --> 00:03:30,454
and their ability to work.

114
00:03:30,634 --> 00:03:32,064
When the economy grows,

115
00:03:32,064 --> 00:03:34,239
owners tend to capture more of that

116
00:03:34,239 --> 00:03:34,675
growth.

117
00:03:35,024 --> 00:03:36,882
And look at retirement accounts.

118
00:03:37,062 --> 00:03:39,756
Vanguard's How America Saves report found the

119
00:03:39,756 --> 00:03:42,329
average balance in the plans it administers

120
00:03:42,329 --> 00:03:44,903
was around one hundred forty eight thousand

121
00:03:44,903 --> 00:03:46,699
dollars in twenty twenty four,

122
00:03:46,699 --> 00:03:48,854
but the median was only about thirty

123
00:03:48,854 --> 00:03:49,752
eight thousand.

124
00:03:49,932 --> 00:03:51,045
Like net worth,

125
00:03:51,045 --> 00:03:53,939
a smaller group with big balances pulls

126
00:03:53,939 --> 00:03:55,052
the average up.

127
00:03:55,232 --> 00:03:57,893
The typical worker has far less invested

128
00:03:57,893 --> 00:03:59,690
than the headlines suggest.

129
00:04:00,039 --> 00:04:01,626
Put it together and you get the

130
00:04:01,626 --> 00:04:02,547
shape of the trap.

131
00:04:02,727 --> 00:04:05,014
A good income that mostly gets spent.

132
00:04:05,194 --> 00:04:07,039
A house that holds most of the

133
00:04:07,039 --> 00:04:07,470
wealth.

134
00:04:07,650 --> 00:04:09,449
A small amount invested.

135
00:04:09,629 --> 00:04:11,946
And a monthly budget full of payments

136
00:04:11,946 --> 00:04:14,200
that leave little room to change any

137
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:14,575
of it.

138
00:04:14,755 --> 00:04:16,809
Now let's go through the fifteen traps

139
00:04:16,809 --> 00:04:18,053
that create that shape.

140
00:04:18,402 --> 00:04:20,132
There's also a timing problem.

141
00:04:20,312 --> 00:04:22,728
Middle class costs tend to peak at

142
00:04:22,728 --> 00:04:23,722
the same time:

143
00:04:23,722 --> 00:04:25,285
a mortgage, car loans,

144
00:04:25,285 --> 00:04:28,625
child care, kids' activities and sometimes help

145
00:04:28,625 --> 00:04:29,903
for aging parents,

146
00:04:29,903 --> 00:04:32,816
all landing in your thirties and forties.

147
00:04:32,996 --> 00:04:35,142
Those are also the years when investing

148
00:04:35,142 --> 00:04:36,572
has the most time to grow.

149
00:04:36,752 --> 00:04:38,992
So the decades when money could compound

150
00:04:38,992 --> 00:04:40,953
the most are often the decades when

151
00:04:40,953 --> 00:04:42,801
there's the least left to invest.

152
00:04:42,981 --> 00:04:45,174
That collision is one of the quiet

153
00:04:45,174 --> 00:04:46,464
engines of the trap.

154
00:04:46,813 --> 00:04:49,716
And retirement has shifted onto workers' shoulders.

155
00:04:49,896 --> 00:04:52,493
Decades ago, many middle class workers had

156
00:04:52,493 --> 00:04:55,585
traditional pensions that promised a monthly check

157
00:04:55,585 --> 00:04:56,142
for life.

158
00:04:56,322 --> 00:04:59,369
Today, most private sector workers rely on

159
00:04:59,369 --> 00:05:00,602
401k style plans,

160
00:05:00,602 --> 00:05:03,504
where the amount you retire with depends

161
00:05:03,504 --> 00:05:05,172
on how much you put in,

162
00:05:05,172 --> 00:05:07,566
how it's invested and how long it

163
00:05:07,566 --> 00:05:08,002
grows.

164
00:05:08,182 --> 00:05:09,629
That's more flexible,

165
00:05:09,629 --> 00:05:11,627
but it means the trap now has

166
00:05:11,627 --> 00:05:12,591
a direct cost:

167
00:05:12,591 --> 00:05:14,451
if you don't invest enough,

168
00:05:14,451 --> 00:05:16,587
nobody else will do it for you.

169
00:05:16,937 --> 00:05:19,325
Trap number one is the one almost

170
00:05:19,325 --> 00:05:22,002
every middle class family falls into,

171
00:05:22,002 --> 00:05:24,101
and it's the place they live.

172
00:05:24,450 --> 00:05:25,635
Trap number one.

173
00:05:25,815 --> 00:05:27,649
The house is the only real asset.

174
00:05:27,999 --> 00:05:29,346
For many families,

175
00:05:29,346 --> 00:05:31,816
the house is the retirement plan,

176
00:05:31,816 --> 00:05:35,408
the emergency fund and the investment portfolio,

177
00:05:35,408 --> 00:05:36,231
all in one.

178
00:05:36,411 --> 00:05:38,566
They pour money into the mortgage,

179
00:05:38,566 --> 00:05:40,659
the renovations and the upgrades,

180
00:05:40,659 --> 00:05:42,878
and very little into anything else.

181
00:05:43,058 --> 00:05:45,449
On paper, their net worth looks healthy.

182
00:05:45,629 --> 00:05:46,469
In real life,

183
00:05:46,469 --> 00:05:48,276
they can't touch most of it.

184
00:05:48,626 --> 00:05:50,830
A house is a fine thing to

185
00:05:50,830 --> 00:05:51,169
own.

186
00:05:51,349 --> 00:05:52,771
But it doesn't pay you income while

187
00:05:52,771 --> 00:05:53,381
you live in it.

188
00:05:53,561 --> 00:05:55,863
It costs you money every year in

189
00:05:55,863 --> 00:05:57,949
taxes, insurance and repairs.

190
00:05:58,129 --> 00:05:59,821
And to unlock its value,

191
00:05:59,821 --> 00:06:01,231
you have to sell it,

192
00:06:01,231 --> 00:06:02,500
borrow against it,

193
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:03,064
or move.

194
00:06:03,244 --> 00:06:06,001
A family with four hundred thousand dollars

195
00:06:06,001 --> 00:06:08,758
of home equity and twenty thousand invested

196
00:06:08,758 --> 00:06:10,875
is much less free than the number

197
00:06:10,875 --> 00:06:11,452
suggests.

198
00:06:11,801 --> 00:06:13,391
The fix: track two numbers,

199
00:06:13,391 --> 00:06:15,333
your net worth with the house and

200
00:06:15,333 --> 00:06:15,981
without it.

201
00:06:16,161 --> 00:06:18,415
Aim to grow the second number steadily,

202
00:06:18,415 --> 00:06:21,304
through retirement accounts and other investments.

203
00:06:21,484 --> 00:06:23,229
Your home is part of your wealth,

204
00:06:23,229 --> 00:06:23,969
not all of it.

205
00:06:24,318 --> 00:06:25,654
Trap number two.

206
00:06:25,834 --> 00:06:27,215
Buying too much house.

207
00:06:27,565 --> 00:06:29,523
Lenders will often approve you for more

208
00:06:29,523 --> 00:06:30,677
than you should borrow.

209
00:06:30,857 --> 00:06:32,664
And when you're approved for a bigger

210
00:06:32,664 --> 00:06:34,177
house, it's tempting to buy it.

211
00:06:34,357 --> 00:06:35,715
A bigger mortgage,

212
00:06:35,715 --> 00:06:37,374
bigger property taxes,

213
00:06:37,374 --> 00:06:40,315
bigger insurance, bigger utility bills,

214
00:06:40,315 --> 00:06:42,275
and more rooms to furnish.

215
00:06:42,455 --> 00:06:44,946
Each one locks in higher costs for

216
00:06:44,946 --> 00:06:45,532
decades.

217
00:06:45,881 --> 00:06:48,890
Harvard's Joint Center for Housing Studies reports

218
00:06:48,890 --> 00:06:51,598
that millions of American households are cost

219
00:06:51,598 --> 00:06:54,306
burdened, meaning they spend more than thirty

220
00:06:54,306 --> 00:06:56,412
percent of their income on housing.

221
00:06:56,592 --> 00:06:58,531
When housing takes that much,

222
00:06:58,531 --> 00:07:01,073
saving and investing get squeezed out.

223
00:07:01,253 --> 00:07:03,520
The house becomes the reason you can't

224
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:05,131
build wealth anywhere else.

225
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:07,919
The fix: keep your total housing cost,

226
00:07:07,919 --> 00:07:09,844
including taxes and insurance,

227
00:07:09,844 --> 00:07:11,961
well below what the bank says you

228
00:07:11,961 --> 00:07:12,667
can afford.

229
00:07:12,847 --> 00:07:16,060
Many planners suggest staying around twenty five

230
00:07:16,060 --> 00:07:18,604
to thirty percent of your gross income

231
00:07:18,604 --> 00:07:19,140
or less.

232
00:07:19,320 --> 00:07:21,453
The difference between a good house and

233
00:07:21,453 --> 00:07:23,367
a dream house can be the difference

234
00:07:23,367 --> 00:07:24,625
between stuck and free.

235
00:07:24,975 --> 00:07:26,264
Trap number three.

236
00:07:26,444 --> 00:07:27,872
The permanent car payment.

237
00:07:28,222 --> 00:07:30,580
Many middle class families always have a

238
00:07:30,580 --> 00:07:31,287
car payment.

239
00:07:31,467 --> 00:07:32,736
When one loan ends,

240
00:07:32,736 --> 00:07:34,538
they trade in for a new car

241
00:07:34,538 --> 00:07:35,740
and start another.

242
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:38,373
Kelley Blue Book has reported average new

243
00:07:38,373 --> 00:07:40,946
vehicle prices near fifty thousand dollars,

244
00:07:40,946 --> 00:07:43,340
and Edmunds has reported average new car

245
00:07:43,340 --> 00:07:46,032
payments above seven hundred dollars a month.

246
00:07:46,382 --> 00:07:48,132
Seven hundred dollars a month,

247
00:07:48,132 --> 00:07:50,874
forever, is eight thousand four hundred dollars

248
00:07:50,874 --> 00:07:51,282
a year.

249
00:07:51,462 --> 00:07:52,471
Over thirty years,

250
00:07:52,471 --> 00:07:54,265
at a seven percent illustration,

251
00:07:54,265 --> 00:07:56,563
that money invested could grow to roughly

252
00:07:56,563 --> 00:07:58,637
eight hundred fifty thousand dollars.

253
00:07:58,817 --> 00:08:00,422
For a two car household,

254
00:08:00,422 --> 00:08:03,232
the permanent car payment can quietly cost

255
00:08:03,232 --> 00:08:05,574
a family a seven figure retirement.

256
00:08:05,923 --> 00:08:07,623
The fix: buy reliable cars,

257
00:08:07,623 --> 00:08:10,015
keep them long after they're paid off,

258
00:08:10,015 --> 00:08:11,463
and when the loan ends,

259
00:08:11,463 --> 00:08:12,973
keep making the payment,

260
00:08:12,973 --> 00:08:15,177
but to your own investment account.

261
00:08:15,357 --> 00:08:17,148
Every year you drive a paid off

262
00:08:17,148 --> 00:08:19,228
car is a year you're paying yourself

263
00:08:19,228 --> 00:08:20,384
instead of a lender.

264
00:08:20,733 --> 00:08:21,964
Trap number four.

265
00:08:22,144 --> 00:08:25,092
Your lifestyle rises exactly as fast as

266
00:08:25,092 --> 00:08:25,999
your income.

267
00:08:26,348 --> 00:08:28,591
When middle class incomes rise,

268
00:08:28,591 --> 00:08:30,400
spending usually follows.

269
00:08:30,580 --> 00:08:32,531
The apartment becomes a house.

270
00:08:32,711 --> 00:08:34,557
The house gets a renovation.

271
00:08:34,737 --> 00:08:36,385
The vacations get nicer.

272
00:08:36,565 --> 00:08:37,599
None of it is wrong.

273
00:08:37,779 --> 00:08:39,909
But if spending rises as fast as

274
00:08:39,909 --> 00:08:42,572
income, your savings rate never changes,

275
00:08:42,572 --> 00:08:45,302
and neither does your financial position.

276
00:08:45,651 --> 00:08:47,657
This is the core mechanism of the

277
00:08:47,657 --> 00:08:48,751
middle class trap.

278
00:08:48,931 --> 00:08:51,220
Two families can earn the same raises

279
00:08:51,220 --> 00:08:52,333
over twenty years.

280
00:08:52,513 --> 00:08:54,727
The one that spends every raise ends

281
00:08:54,727 --> 00:08:56,694
up with a nicer lifestyle and no

282
00:08:56,694 --> 00:08:57,494
extra wealth.

283
00:08:57,674 --> 00:08:59,579
The one that saves half of every

284
00:08:59,579 --> 00:09:01,484
raise ends up with both a better

285
00:09:01,484 --> 00:09:03,746
lifestyle and a much larger net worth.

286
00:09:04,095 --> 00:09:06,419
The fix: decide in advance that at

287
00:09:06,419 --> 00:09:08,675
least half of every raise goes to

288
00:09:08,675 --> 00:09:10,179
savings and investing.

289
00:09:10,359 --> 00:09:12,673
Automate it the moment the raise arrives,

290
00:09:12,673 --> 00:09:14,479
before you get used to the extra

291
00:09:14,479 --> 00:09:14,817
money.

292
00:09:14,997 --> 00:09:17,167
Your life still improves every year,

293
00:09:17,167 --> 00:09:19,096
just not as fast as your income.

294
00:09:19,445 --> 00:09:20,920
Trap number five.

295
00:09:21,100 --> 00:09:22,958
Saving, but not investing.

296
00:09:23,307 --> 00:09:25,923
Some middle class families are good savers,

297
00:09:25,923 --> 00:09:28,235
but they keep everything in a checking

298
00:09:28,235 --> 00:09:29,391
or savings account.

299
00:09:29,571 --> 00:09:30,848
It feels safe.

300
00:09:31,028 --> 00:09:33,318
But money sitting in cash for decades

301
00:09:33,318 --> 00:09:35,298
loses buying power to inflation,

302
00:09:35,298 --> 00:09:37,341
and misses out on the growth that

303
00:09:37,341 --> 00:09:38,517
builds real wealth.

304
00:09:38,866 --> 00:09:40,614
At three percent inflation,

305
00:09:40,614 --> 00:09:43,331
prices roughly double in about twenty four

306
00:09:43,331 --> 00:09:43,719
years.

307
00:09:43,899 --> 00:09:46,068
So money that just sits still is

308
00:09:46,068 --> 00:09:47,626
slowly getting smaller.

309
00:09:47,806 --> 00:09:51,123
Meanwhile, broad stock markets have historically grown

310
00:09:51,123 --> 00:09:53,580
faster than inflation over long periods,

311
00:09:53,580 --> 00:09:55,730
though with ups and downs along the

312
00:09:55,730 --> 00:09:57,942
way and no guarantee for the future.

313
00:09:58,291 --> 00:10:00,881
The fix: keep your emergency fund in

314
00:10:00,881 --> 00:10:02,680
safe, high yield savings.

315
00:10:02,860 --> 00:10:05,471
Invest money you won't need for many

316
00:10:05,471 --> 00:10:06,994
years in diversified,

317
00:10:06,994 --> 00:10:08,082
low cost funds,

318
00:10:08,082 --> 00:10:10,476
in tax advantaged accounts first.

319
00:10:10,656 --> 00:10:12,328
Saving is the first step.

320
00:10:12,508 --> 00:10:15,550
Investing is what turns savings into wealth.

321
00:10:15,899 --> 00:10:17,397
Trap number six.

322
00:10:17,577 --> 00:10:19,713
Investing only enough to get the match.

323
00:10:20,063 --> 00:10:23,004
Many workers contribute exactly enough to get

324
00:10:23,004 --> 00:10:24,376
their employer match,

325
00:10:24,376 --> 00:10:26,663
often around four to six percent of

326
00:10:26,663 --> 00:10:27,970
pay, and stop there.

327
00:10:28,150 --> 00:10:29,508
Getting the match is smart.

328
00:10:29,688 --> 00:10:30,788
But for most people,

329
00:10:30,788 --> 00:10:32,878
it's not enough to build a comfortable

330
00:10:32,878 --> 00:10:34,088
retirement on its own.

331
00:10:34,438 --> 00:10:38,190
Many retirement planners suggest saving around fifteen

332
00:10:38,190 --> 00:10:39,440
percent of income,

333
00:10:39,440 --> 00:10:41,455
including any employer match,

334
00:10:41,455 --> 00:10:42,774
over a full career.

335
00:10:42,954 --> 00:10:45,535
Someone saving six percent total may end

336
00:10:45,535 --> 00:10:47,729
up with a fraction of what they'll

337
00:10:47,729 --> 00:10:48,051
need.

338
00:10:48,231 --> 00:10:49,566
The match is the floor,

339
00:10:49,566 --> 00:10:50,321
not the goal.

340
00:10:50,670 --> 00:10:53,248
The fix: raise your contribution by one

341
00:10:53,248 --> 00:10:55,098
percentage point every year,

342
00:10:55,098 --> 00:10:57,477
ideally timed with your raise so you

343
00:10:57,477 --> 00:10:58,403
never feel it.

344
00:10:58,583 --> 00:11:01,323
Many plans offer an automatic increase feature

345
00:11:01,323 --> 00:11:02,693
that does this for you.

346
00:11:02,873 --> 00:11:03,828
In a few years,

347
00:11:03,828 --> 00:11:06,441
you'll be at fifteen percent without ever

348
00:11:06,441 --> 00:11:08,097
having made a painful cut.

349
00:11:08,447 --> 00:11:09,852
Trap number seven.

350
00:11:10,032 --> 00:11:11,913
An income that stops growing.

351
00:11:12,263 --> 00:11:14,724
Many middle class workers stay in the

352
00:11:14,724 --> 00:11:15,987
same job for years,

353
00:11:15,987 --> 00:11:18,913
getting small annual raises that barely beat

354
00:11:18,913 --> 00:11:19,578
inflation.

355
00:11:19,758 --> 00:11:21,557
Loyalty feels safe.

356
00:11:21,737 --> 00:11:24,173
But wage data from the Federal Reserve

357
00:11:24,173 --> 00:11:26,481
Bank of Atlanta has often shown that

358
00:11:26,481 --> 00:11:28,853
people who switch jobs see faster pay

359
00:11:28,853 --> 00:11:30,584
growth than those who stay.

360
00:11:30,934 --> 00:11:32,176
When income stalls,

361
00:11:32,176 --> 00:11:34,332
every other part of the plan gets

362
00:11:34,332 --> 00:11:34,789
harder.

363
00:11:34,969 --> 00:11:36,877
It's the one input that can grow

364
00:11:36,877 --> 00:11:38,785
without limit, and it compounds,

365
00:11:38,785 --> 00:11:41,110
because future raises are based on your

366
00:11:41,110 --> 00:11:42,005
current salary.

367
00:11:42,185 --> 00:11:44,010
A few years of stalled pay can

368
00:11:44,010 --> 00:11:46,504
cost far more than any budgeting mistake.

369
00:11:46,853 --> 00:11:49,071
The fix: know your market value.

370
00:11:49,251 --> 00:11:51,580
Check job listings for your role every

371
00:11:51,580 --> 00:11:51,886
year.

372
00:11:52,066 --> 00:11:54,087
Learn one skill that's in demand.

373
00:11:54,267 --> 00:11:56,147
Ask for raises with evidence.

374
00:11:56,327 --> 00:11:57,726
And be willing to move,

375
00:11:57,726 --> 00:11:59,612
inside your company or outside,

376
00:11:59,612 --> 00:12:01,923
when the pay difference is meaningful.

377
00:12:02,273 --> 00:12:03,516
Trap number eight.

378
00:12:03,696 --> 00:12:06,097
Funding everything for the kids before your

379
00:12:06,097 --> 00:12:06,935
own retirement.

380
00:12:07,284 --> 00:12:10,555
Middle class parents often put their children

381
00:12:10,555 --> 00:12:12,444
first: activities, sports,

382
00:12:12,444 --> 00:12:14,843
private lessons, college savings.

383
00:12:15,023 --> 00:12:17,535
Child Care Aware of America reports that

384
00:12:17,535 --> 00:12:20,048
the national average price of child care

385
00:12:20,048 --> 00:12:22,687
has been more than eleven thousand dollars

386
00:12:22,687 --> 00:12:23,126
a year.

387
00:12:23,306 --> 00:12:24,650
These costs are real,

388
00:12:24,650 --> 00:12:26,186
and they come from love.

389
00:12:26,536 --> 00:12:28,898
But here's the hard truth many financial

390
00:12:28,898 --> 00:12:31,320
planners repeat: your kids can borrow for

391
00:12:31,320 --> 00:12:33,978
college, but you can't borrow for retirement.

392
00:12:34,158 --> 00:12:36,172
If you reach sixty with no savings,

393
00:12:36,172 --> 00:12:38,244
your kids may end up supporting you.

394
00:12:38,424 --> 00:12:40,286
Securing your own future can be one

395
00:12:40,286 --> 00:12:41,988
of the best gifts you give them.

396
00:12:42,338 --> 00:12:44,595
The fix: fund your own retirement first,

397
00:12:44,595 --> 00:12:46,400
at least to the point of getting

398
00:12:46,400 --> 00:12:48,430
the full match and a healthy savings

399
00:12:48,430 --> 00:12:48,712
rate.

400
00:12:48,892 --> 00:12:52,509
Then save for college with what's left,

401
00:12:52,509 --> 00:12:55,754
often in a tax advantaged 529 plan.

402
00:12:55,934 --> 00:12:58,070
And be selective about activities.

403
00:12:58,250 --> 00:13:00,017
Kids need your time more than your

404
00:13:00,017 --> 00:13:00,328
money.

405
00:13:00,678 --> 00:13:01,874
Trap number nine.

406
00:13:02,054 --> 00:13:04,516
Carrying credit card debt month to month.

407
00:13:04,865 --> 00:13:07,229
Credit card interest rates have been above

408
00:13:07,229 --> 00:13:09,985
twenty percent, according to the Federal Reserve.

409
00:13:10,165 --> 00:13:12,906
Many middle class families carry a balance

410
00:13:12,906 --> 00:13:14,211
from month to month,

411
00:13:14,211 --> 00:13:16,233
using the card to cover the gap

412
00:13:16,233 --> 00:13:18,060
between income and spending.

413
00:13:18,410 --> 00:13:20,734
A ten thousand dollar balance at twenty

414
00:13:20,734 --> 00:13:23,117
two percent costs about two thousand two

415
00:13:23,117 --> 00:13:25,202
hundred dollars a year in interest.

416
00:13:25,382 --> 00:13:27,625
That's money flowing out of your household

417
00:13:27,625 --> 00:13:28,854
to the bank every year,

418
00:13:28,854 --> 00:13:30,456
for things you already bought.

419
00:13:30,636 --> 00:13:33,782
It's compound interest working directly against you.

420
00:13:34,132 --> 00:13:36,389
The fix: stop adding to the balance,

421
00:13:36,389 --> 00:13:39,023
build a small cushion so emergencies don't

422
00:13:39,023 --> 00:13:40,088
land on the card,

423
00:13:40,088 --> 00:13:42,220
then pay off the highest rate debt

424
00:13:42,220 --> 00:13:42,596
first.

425
00:13:42,776 --> 00:13:43,763
Once it's gone,

426
00:13:43,763 --> 00:13:45,934
pay the card in full every month.

427
00:13:46,114 --> 00:13:48,464
Every dollar of interest you stop paying

428
00:13:48,464 --> 00:13:50,050
is a dollar you can invest.

429
00:13:50,399 --> 00:13:51,410
Quick pause.

430
00:13:51,590 --> 00:13:53,423
If you see your family in some

431
00:13:53,423 --> 00:13:54,340
of these traps,

432
00:13:54,340 --> 00:13:55,379
you're not alone,

433
00:13:55,379 --> 00:13:56,663
and you're not stuck.

434
00:13:56,843 --> 00:13:58,299
Subscribe to the channel,

435
00:13:58,299 --> 00:14:00,278
because the next videos go step by

436
00:14:00,278 --> 00:14:02,025
step through how to break out,

437
00:14:02,025 --> 00:14:04,004
from the first ten thousand to the

438
00:14:04,004 --> 00:14:04,819
first million.

439
00:14:04,999 --> 00:14:06,880
Now, trap number ten.

440
00:14:07,229 --> 00:14:08,530
Trap number ten.

441
00:14:08,710 --> 00:14:10,382
High fees on investments.

442
00:14:10,731 --> 00:14:12,387
Some retirement plans,

443
00:14:12,387 --> 00:14:16,151
funds and advisory arrangements charge one percent

444
00:14:16,151 --> 00:14:17,279
a year or more.

445
00:14:17,459 --> 00:14:18,504
It sounds tiny.

446
00:14:18,684 --> 00:14:20,336
But fees come out every year,

447
00:14:20,336 --> 00:14:22,558
whether your investments go up or down,

448
00:14:22,558 --> 00:14:24,780
and they compound just like returns do.

449
00:14:25,129 --> 00:14:26,195
Over thirty years,

450
00:14:26,195 --> 00:14:29,035
the difference between paying around one percent

451
00:14:29,035 --> 00:14:30,928
and paying a small fraction of a

452
00:14:30,928 --> 00:14:32,644
percent can add up to a large

453
00:14:32,644 --> 00:14:34,301
share of your final balance.

454
00:14:34,481 --> 00:14:36,823
On a middle class retirement account,

455
00:14:36,823 --> 00:14:39,102
that can easily be tens of thousands

456
00:14:39,102 --> 00:14:40,368
of dollars, or more,

457
00:14:40,368 --> 00:14:43,153
simply transferred from you to the financial

458
00:14:43,153 --> 00:14:43,723
industry.

459
00:14:44,072 --> 00:14:46,490
The fix: look up the expense ratio

460
00:14:46,490 --> 00:14:48,055
of every fund you own.

461
00:14:48,235 --> 00:14:49,906
In your workplace plan,

462
00:14:49,906 --> 00:14:52,232
look for low cost index funds or

463
00:14:52,232 --> 00:14:53,540
target date funds.

464
00:14:53,720 --> 00:14:55,073
If you pay an advisor,

465
00:14:55,073 --> 00:14:57,287
know exactly what you pay in dollars

466
00:14:57,287 --> 00:14:59,132
per year, and what you get for

467
00:14:59,132 --> 00:14:59,316
it.

468
00:14:59,666 --> 00:15:01,048
Trap number eleven.

469
00:15:01,228 --> 00:15:03,747
Not using tax advantaged accounts.

470
00:15:04,098 --> 00:15:07,262
The tax code gives middle class families

471
00:15:07,262 --> 00:15:10,664
powerful tools: workplace retirement plans,

472
00:15:10,664 --> 00:15:14,541
IRAs, health savings accounts for eligible plans,

473
00:15:14,541 --> 00:15:16,915
and 529 college savings plans.

474
00:15:17,095 --> 00:15:19,402
Each one lets money grow with tax

475
00:15:19,402 --> 00:15:20,172
advantages.

476
00:15:20,352 --> 00:15:22,810
Yet many families don't use them fully,

477
00:15:22,810 --> 00:15:23,440
or at all.

478
00:15:23,790 --> 00:15:26,685
Wealthy families use these tools aggressively,

479
00:15:26,685 --> 00:15:27,818
along with others.

480
00:15:27,998 --> 00:15:30,672
Middle class families often leave them partly

481
00:15:30,672 --> 00:15:31,028
empty.

482
00:15:31,208 --> 00:15:34,277
Over decades, the difference between growing money

483
00:15:34,277 --> 00:15:36,731
with tax advantages and without them can

484
00:15:36,731 --> 00:15:37,652
be substantial.

485
00:15:38,002 --> 00:15:41,032
The fix: learn the basic accounts available

486
00:15:41,032 --> 00:15:43,570
to you, check the current IRS limits

487
00:15:43,570 --> 00:15:46,178
each year, and fill the most valuable

488
00:15:46,178 --> 00:15:46,953
ones first.

489
00:15:47,133 --> 00:15:49,264
Often that's the employer match,

490
00:15:49,264 --> 00:15:52,062
then a health savings account if eligible,

491
00:15:52,062 --> 00:15:52,861
then an IRA,

492
00:15:52,861 --> 00:15:54,993
then more in the workplace plan.

493
00:15:55,343 --> 00:15:56,608
Trap number twelve.

494
00:15:56,788 --> 00:15:58,947
Missing insurance and protection.

495
00:15:59,298 --> 00:16:02,043
Middle class wealth is fragile because it's

496
00:16:02,043 --> 00:16:04,214
often built on a single income and

497
00:16:04,214 --> 00:16:05,172
a single house.

498
00:16:05,352 --> 00:16:07,702
A disability, a serious illness,

499
00:16:07,702 --> 00:16:10,125
a lawsuit or a death without life

500
00:16:10,125 --> 00:16:13,209
insurance can erase decades of progress in

501
00:16:13,209 --> 00:16:13,723
months.

502
00:16:14,073 --> 00:16:16,458
Many people insure their car and their

503
00:16:16,458 --> 00:16:18,655
phone but not their ability to earn

504
00:16:18,655 --> 00:16:21,103
an income, which is often their biggest

505
00:16:21,103 --> 00:16:21,480
asset.

506
00:16:21,660 --> 00:16:24,662
Long term disability insurance and term life

507
00:16:24,662 --> 00:16:27,118
insurance, if anyone depends on you,

508
00:16:27,118 --> 00:16:30,325
protect the foundation everything else is built

509
00:16:30,325 --> 00:16:30,530
on.

510
00:16:30,880 --> 00:16:33,611
The fix: check whether your employer offers

511
00:16:33,611 --> 00:16:36,406
disability insurance and how much it covers.

512
00:16:36,586 --> 00:16:38,650
If people depend on your income,

513
00:16:38,650 --> 00:16:41,230
consider affordable term life insurance.

514
00:16:41,410 --> 00:16:43,970
Write a basic will and name beneficiaries

515
00:16:43,970 --> 00:16:45,032
on every account.

516
00:16:45,212 --> 00:16:47,186
It's boring, and it's essential.

517
00:16:47,536 --> 00:16:49,184
Trap number thirteen.

518
00:16:49,364 --> 00:16:51,988
Selling investments in a market crash.

519
00:16:52,338 --> 00:16:53,904
Every decade or so,

520
00:16:53,904 --> 00:16:55,635
markets fall sharply.

521
00:16:55,815 --> 00:16:58,449
When middle class families finally have some

522
00:16:58,449 --> 00:17:00,425
money invested and watch it drop,

523
00:17:00,425 --> 00:17:01,922
the fear is overwhelming.

524
00:17:02,102 --> 00:17:03,755
Many sell near the bottom,

525
00:17:03,755 --> 00:17:06,235
then wait on the sidelines until prices

526
00:17:06,235 --> 00:17:07,698
have already recovered.

527
00:17:08,048 --> 00:17:11,102
Research firms like DALBAR have repeatedly found

528
00:17:11,102 --> 00:17:13,711
that the average investor earns less than

529
00:17:13,711 --> 00:17:15,302
the funds they invest in,

530
00:17:15,302 --> 00:17:17,975
largely because of buying high and selling

531
00:17:17,975 --> 00:17:18,230
low.

532
00:17:18,410 --> 00:17:21,013
Panic turns a temporary decline into a

533
00:17:21,013 --> 00:17:23,205
permanent loss, and it can set a

534
00:17:23,205 --> 00:17:24,644
family back by years.

535
00:17:24,994 --> 00:17:27,281
The fix: decide now what you'll do

536
00:17:27,281 --> 00:17:28,020
in a crash,

537
00:17:28,020 --> 00:17:29,231
and write it down.

538
00:17:29,411 --> 00:17:31,356
For most long term investors,

539
00:17:31,356 --> 00:17:33,904
the answer is to keep contributing and

540
00:17:33,904 --> 00:17:34,508
not sell.

541
00:17:34,688 --> 00:17:37,208
Keep your emergency fund separate so you're

542
00:17:37,208 --> 00:17:38,438
never forced to sell.

543
00:17:38,618 --> 00:17:40,220
And check your accounts less often.

544
00:17:40,571 --> 00:17:42,126
Trap number fourteen.

545
00:17:42,306 --> 00:17:43,972
Relying only on your labor,

546
00:17:43,972 --> 00:17:45,453
and never owning assets.

547
00:17:45,803 --> 00:17:48,379
Most middle class income comes from one

548
00:17:48,379 --> 00:17:49,634
source: a paycheck.

549
00:17:49,814 --> 00:17:51,366
When you stop working,

550
00:17:51,366 --> 00:17:52,565
the income stops.

551
00:17:52,745 --> 00:17:55,350
Wealthier families earn a large share of

552
00:17:55,350 --> 00:17:57,433
their income from what they own:

553
00:17:57,433 --> 00:17:59,711
stocks, businesses and real estate.

554
00:17:59,891 --> 00:18:01,877
Their money works even when they don't.

555
00:18:02,227 --> 00:18:04,568
This is the deepest reason the trap

556
00:18:04,568 --> 00:18:05,036
exists.

557
00:18:05,216 --> 00:18:07,062
Wages tend to grow slowly.

558
00:18:07,242 --> 00:18:08,810
Ownership can compound.

559
00:18:08,990 --> 00:18:10,704
The goal isn't to stop working,

560
00:18:10,704 --> 00:18:13,302
it's to gradually shift from earning everything

561
00:18:13,302 --> 00:18:15,293
with your time to having your assets

562
00:18:15,293 --> 00:18:16,675
earn more and more of it.

563
00:18:17,026 --> 00:18:18,759
The fix: become an owner,

564
00:18:18,759 --> 00:18:19,661
step by step.

565
00:18:19,841 --> 00:18:21,816
Broad index funds make you a part

566
00:18:21,816 --> 00:18:23,730
owner of thousands of companies.

567
00:18:23,910 --> 00:18:26,370
Some people add rental property or a

568
00:18:26,370 --> 00:18:28,078
small business over time.

569
00:18:28,258 --> 00:18:30,366
Every dollar you invest is a small

570
00:18:30,366 --> 00:18:31,916
employee working for you.

571
00:18:32,266 --> 00:18:33,926
Trap number fifteen.

572
00:18:34,106 --> 00:18:35,592
The most expensive one.

573
00:18:35,772 --> 00:18:38,407
Waiting to get serious until later.

574
00:18:38,758 --> 00:18:40,847
Later is when the kids are older.

575
00:18:41,027 --> 00:18:42,757
When the car is paid off.

576
00:18:42,937 --> 00:18:44,563
When the next raise comes.

577
00:18:44,743 --> 00:18:47,158
Middle class life always has a reason

578
00:18:47,158 --> 00:18:47,680
to wait.

579
00:18:47,860 --> 00:18:50,155
And every year of waiting costs more

580
00:18:50,155 --> 00:18:51,047
than the last,

581
00:18:51,047 --> 00:18:53,788
because you lose the years when compounding

582
00:18:53,788 --> 00:18:54,489
works best.

583
00:18:54,840 --> 00:18:56,789
At a seven percent illustration,

584
00:18:56,789 --> 00:18:59,164
five hundred dollars a month for thirty

585
00:18:59,164 --> 00:19:01,478
years grows to roughly six hundred ten

586
00:19:01,478 --> 00:19:02,514
thousand dollars.

587
00:19:02,694 --> 00:19:04,885
Wait ten years and invest the same

588
00:19:04,885 --> 00:19:06,754
amount for only twenty years,

589
00:19:06,754 --> 00:19:09,204
and you'd end with roughly two hundred

590
00:19:09,204 --> 00:19:10,171
sixty thousand.

591
00:19:10,351 --> 00:19:12,491
Ten years of waiting can cost more

592
00:19:12,491 --> 00:19:14,379
than half of the final result.

593
00:19:14,730 --> 00:19:16,059
The fix: start now,

594
00:19:16,059 --> 00:19:17,597
with whatever you can.

595
00:19:17,777 --> 00:19:19,255
Even a small amount,

596
00:19:19,255 --> 00:19:22,358
automated today, beats a perfect plan that

597
00:19:22,358 --> 00:19:23,466
starts someday.

598
00:19:23,646 --> 00:19:25,132
Then increase it every year.

599
00:19:25,312 --> 00:19:27,251
The best time was ten years ago.

600
00:19:27,431 --> 00:19:29,556
The second best time is this week.

601
00:19:29,906 --> 00:19:31,413
How do you know if your family

602
00:19:31,413 --> 00:19:32,820
is in the middle class trap?

603
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:34,718
Here are eight warning signs.

604
00:19:34,898 --> 00:19:36,419
Count how many apply to you.

605
00:19:36,769 --> 00:19:37,523
Sign one.

606
00:19:37,703 --> 00:19:39,515
Your income has gone up a lot

607
00:19:39,515 --> 00:19:41,015
over the past ten years,

608
00:19:41,015 --> 00:19:43,264
but your savings haven't grown much.

609
00:19:43,444 --> 00:19:44,304
Sign two.

610
00:19:44,484 --> 00:19:46,318
You've had a car payment for most

611
00:19:46,318 --> 00:19:47,374
of your adult life,

612
00:19:47,374 --> 00:19:49,375
and you can't remember the last time

613
00:19:49,375 --> 00:19:49,987
you didn't.

614
00:19:50,167 --> 00:19:51,154
Sign three.

615
00:19:51,334 --> 00:19:53,306
Your home equity is more than three

616
00:19:53,306 --> 00:19:54,828
quarters of your net worth.

617
00:19:55,178 --> 00:19:55,955
Sign four.

618
00:19:56,135 --> 00:19:59,078
You contribute exactly enough to your retirement

619
00:19:59,078 --> 00:20:00,427
plan to get the match,

620
00:20:00,427 --> 00:20:01,163
and no more.

621
00:20:01,343 --> 00:20:02,306
Sign five.

622
00:20:02,486 --> 00:20:04,552
You carry a credit card balance more

623
00:20:04,552 --> 00:20:05,470
months than not.

624
00:20:05,650 --> 00:20:06,509
Sign six.

625
00:20:06,689 --> 00:20:07,810
If you lost your job,

626
00:20:07,810 --> 00:20:09,945
you'd have less than three months before

627
00:20:09,945 --> 00:20:11,066
you needed to borrow.

628
00:20:11,416 --> 00:20:12,333
Sign seven.

629
00:20:12,513 --> 00:20:13,856
You don't know what fees you pay

630
00:20:13,856 --> 00:20:14,695
on your investments.

631
00:20:14,875 --> 00:20:15,862
And sign eight.

632
00:20:16,042 --> 00:20:17,944
When you imagine retiring,

633
00:20:17,944 --> 00:20:19,334
the plan is mostly,

634
00:20:19,334 --> 00:20:20,797
we'll figure it out,

635
00:20:20,797 --> 00:20:22,625
or, we'll sell the house.

636
00:20:22,805 --> 00:20:24,815
If three or more of these sound

637
00:20:24,815 --> 00:20:26,890
familiar, the trap has a grip on

638
00:20:26,890 --> 00:20:27,797
your finances.

639
00:20:27,977 --> 00:20:29,919
The good news is that every sign

640
00:20:29,919 --> 00:20:31,679
on this list can be reversed,

641
00:20:31,679 --> 00:20:33,318
usually within a few years.

642
00:20:33,668 --> 00:20:35,560
And here's the encouraging part.

643
00:20:35,740 --> 00:20:38,371
Families who reverse just two or three

644
00:20:38,371 --> 00:20:40,793
of these signs often see a dramatic

645
00:20:40,793 --> 00:20:41,278
change.

646
00:20:41,458 --> 00:20:43,665
Paying off the cards and stopping the

647
00:20:43,665 --> 00:20:45,753
car payment cycle alone can free up

648
00:20:45,753 --> 00:20:47,841
over a thousand dollars a month for

649
00:20:47,841 --> 00:20:48,796
many households.

650
00:20:48,976 --> 00:20:50,526
Invested over twenty years,

651
00:20:50,526 --> 00:20:53,341
that's the difference between hoping and knowing.

652
00:20:53,691 --> 00:20:55,537
If you counted zero or one sign,

653
00:20:55,537 --> 00:20:58,539
congratulations, you're probably already on your way

654
00:20:58,539 --> 00:20:59,693
through the ceiling.

655
00:20:59,873 --> 00:21:02,104
Your job now is to protect what's

656
00:21:02,104 --> 00:21:04,672
working: keep the savings rate high as

657
00:21:04,672 --> 00:21:05,889
your income grows,

658
00:21:05,889 --> 00:21:06,836
keep fees low,

659
00:21:06,836 --> 00:21:08,728
and don't let a big house or

660
00:21:08,728 --> 00:21:11,297
a new truck reverse years of progress.

661
00:21:11,477 --> 00:21:13,377
Staying out of the trap takes far

662
00:21:13,377 --> 00:21:15,449
less effort than climbing out of it,

663
00:21:15,449 --> 00:21:17,694
but it still takes attention every time

664
00:21:17,694 --> 00:21:19,709
your income or your family changes.

665
00:21:20,059 --> 00:21:21,949
So what does it actually look like

666
00:21:21,949 --> 00:21:24,285
when a middle class family breaks through?

667
00:21:24,465 --> 00:21:26,705
Let's walk through the next milestones.

668
00:21:27,056 --> 00:21:29,524
At five hundred thousand dollars of net

669
00:21:29,524 --> 00:21:31,423
worth, with a good share of it

670
00:21:31,423 --> 00:21:33,575
invested rather than in the house,

671
00:21:33,575 --> 00:21:34,904
life feels different.

672
00:21:35,084 --> 00:21:37,223
A job loss is a setback,

673
00:21:37,223 --> 00:21:38,381
not a crisis.

674
00:21:38,561 --> 00:21:40,778
Investment growth in a good year can

675
00:21:40,778 --> 00:21:42,195
rival a year of saving.

676
00:21:42,375 --> 00:21:43,890
And for the first time,

677
00:21:43,890 --> 00:21:46,722
early or flexible retirement starts to look

678
00:21:46,722 --> 00:21:48,303
like a real possibility,

679
00:21:48,303 --> 00:21:49,225
not a fantasy.

680
00:21:49,575 --> 00:21:51,066
At one million dollars,

681
00:21:51,066 --> 00:21:53,790
especially with the house mostly paid off,

682
00:21:53,790 --> 00:21:57,098
many families reach what planners call optionality.

683
00:21:57,278 --> 00:21:59,068
They can choose work they like,

684
00:21:59,068 --> 00:22:01,435
help their kids without risking their own

685
00:22:01,435 --> 00:22:03,745
future, and handle almost any emergency.

686
00:22:03,925 --> 00:22:06,280
Using the four percent rule of thumb,

687
00:22:06,280 --> 00:22:09,528
seven hundred fifty thousand dollars invested could

688
00:22:09,528 --> 00:22:12,393
support about thirty thousand dollars a year,

689
00:22:12,393 --> 00:22:14,049
on top of Social Security.

690
00:22:14,399 --> 00:22:16,737
And here's the most important thing families

691
00:22:16,737 --> 00:22:18,172
say about breaking through.

692
00:22:18,352 --> 00:22:19,803
It didn't feel dramatic.

693
00:22:19,983 --> 00:22:21,574
There was no single moment.

694
00:22:21,754 --> 00:22:23,885
It was the same boring choices,

695
00:22:23,885 --> 00:22:25,192
made over and over:

696
00:22:25,192 --> 00:22:26,361
keeping the cars,

697
00:22:26,361 --> 00:22:27,805
investing the raises,

698
00:22:27,805 --> 00:22:29,455
not upgrading the house,

699
00:22:29,455 --> 00:22:31,518
staying invested in the crash.

700
00:22:31,698 --> 00:22:33,917
The trap is broken by habits,

701
00:22:33,917 --> 00:22:35,065
not by heroics.

702
00:22:35,415 --> 00:22:37,532
Let's see how these traps play out

703
00:22:37,532 --> 00:22:38,155
over time.

704
00:22:38,335 --> 00:22:39,844
Two example families.

705
00:22:40,024 --> 00:22:41,930
Same income, same city,

706
00:22:41,930 --> 00:22:43,588
same starting point.

707
00:22:43,768 --> 00:22:45,835
Made up, but the math is real.

708
00:22:46,185 --> 00:22:48,649
The Millers earn ninety thousand dollars a

709
00:22:48,649 --> 00:22:49,470
year combined.

710
00:22:49,650 --> 00:22:51,627
They buy the biggest house the bank

711
00:22:51,627 --> 00:22:52,135
approves.

712
00:22:52,315 --> 00:22:54,163
They always have two car payments,

713
00:22:54,163 --> 00:22:56,773
about thirteen hundred dollars a month together.

714
00:22:56,953 --> 00:22:59,777
They contribute five percent to their 401ks

715
00:22:59,777 --> 00:23:00,894
to get the match,

716
00:23:00,894 --> 00:23:03,652
and their spending rises with every raise.

717
00:23:03,832 --> 00:23:04,900
They're not reckless.

718
00:23:05,080 --> 00:23:05,893
They're normal.

719
00:23:06,243 --> 00:23:08,681
The Parkers earn the same ninety thousand.

720
00:23:08,861 --> 00:23:10,340
They buy a smaller house,

721
00:23:10,340 --> 00:23:11,879
comfortably within budget.

722
00:23:12,059 --> 00:23:14,428
They drive paid off cars for years

723
00:23:14,428 --> 00:23:17,144
and buy reliable used ones when needed.

724
00:23:17,324 --> 00:23:19,957
They save fifteen percent for retirement,

725
00:23:19,957 --> 00:23:21,240
including the match,

726
00:23:21,240 --> 00:23:23,231
and send half of every raise to

727
00:23:23,231 --> 00:23:23,873
investing.

728
00:23:24,053 --> 00:23:26,207
They take vacations and enjoy life,

729
00:23:26,207 --> 00:23:28,546
just without the big monthly payments.

730
00:23:28,896 --> 00:23:30,390
After twenty five years,

731
00:23:30,390 --> 00:23:33,191
both families have paid down their mortgages.

732
00:23:33,371 --> 00:23:35,840
But the Parkers have been investing roughly

733
00:23:35,840 --> 00:23:38,021
an extra thousand dollars a month that

734
00:23:38,021 --> 00:23:39,800
the Millers spent on cars and a

735
00:23:39,800 --> 00:23:40,546
bigger house.

736
00:23:40,726 --> 00:23:42,659
At a seven percent illustration,

737
00:23:42,659 --> 00:23:45,015
one thousand dollars a month for twenty

738
00:23:45,015 --> 00:23:47,492
five years grows to roughly eight hundred

739
00:23:47,492 --> 00:23:48,760
ten thousand dollars.

740
00:23:48,940 --> 00:23:51,666
That difference alone could separate a family

741
00:23:51,666 --> 00:23:54,209
stuck near two hundred fifty thousand from

742
00:23:54,209 --> 00:23:55,663
one well past a million.

743
00:23:56,013 --> 00:23:57,127
Here's what's striking.

744
00:23:57,307 --> 00:23:58,426
From the outside,

745
00:23:58,426 --> 00:24:01,255
the Millers looked more successful for most

746
00:24:01,255 --> 00:24:02,241
of those years.

747
00:24:02,421 --> 00:24:04,360
Bigger house, newer cars.

748
00:24:04,540 --> 00:24:06,235
The Parkers looked ordinary.

749
00:24:06,415 --> 00:24:07,921
But in their fifties,

750
00:24:07,921 --> 00:24:09,714
the Parkers have choices:

751
00:24:09,714 --> 00:24:11,793
retire early, work part time,

752
00:24:11,793 --> 00:24:12,940
help their kids.

753
00:24:13,120 --> 00:24:14,994
The Millers have a big house and

754
00:24:14,994 --> 00:24:15,872
a lot of worry.

755
00:24:16,052 --> 00:24:18,008
That's the middle class trap in one

756
00:24:18,008 --> 00:24:18,455
picture.

757
00:24:18,805 --> 00:24:19,803
Angle two.

758
00:24:19,983 --> 00:24:21,098
The psychology.

759
00:24:21,278 --> 00:24:22,160
Why do smart,

760
00:24:22,160 --> 00:24:24,603
hardworking people stay in the trap,

761
00:24:24,603 --> 00:24:26,572
even when they know the math?

762
00:24:26,922 --> 00:24:29,162
The first reason is social norms.

763
00:24:29,342 --> 00:24:31,636
In most middle class neighborhoods,

764
00:24:31,636 --> 00:24:32,553
the big house,

765
00:24:32,553 --> 00:24:35,239
the new cars and the expensive activities

766
00:24:35,239 --> 00:24:35,960
are normal.

767
00:24:36,140 --> 00:24:38,647
Doing less can feel like falling behind,

768
00:24:38,647 --> 00:24:40,714
or even like failing your family.

769
00:24:40,894 --> 00:24:43,396
Economists have long noted that people judge

770
00:24:43,396 --> 00:24:45,670
their own well being partly by comparing

771
00:24:45,670 --> 00:24:47,489
themselves to those around them.

772
00:24:47,839 --> 00:24:50,250
The second reason is that payments hide

773
00:24:50,250 --> 00:24:50,683
prices.

774
00:24:50,863 --> 00:24:53,394
A fifty thousand dollar truck is scary.

775
00:24:53,574 --> 00:24:56,302
Seven hundred dollars a month feels manageable.

776
00:24:56,482 --> 00:24:58,235
A bigger mortgage is just a few

777
00:24:58,235 --> 00:24:59,536
hundred more per month.

778
00:24:59,716 --> 00:25:02,746
Monthly payments make big decisions feel small,

779
00:25:02,746 --> 00:25:05,390
which is exactly why they're so effective

780
00:25:05,390 --> 00:25:06,937
at keeping people stuck.

781
00:25:07,288 --> 00:25:09,240
The third reason is that the trap

782
00:25:09,240 --> 00:25:10,364
feels like success.

783
00:25:10,544 --> 00:25:12,511
The middle class markers,

784
00:25:12,511 --> 00:25:13,612
a house, cars,

785
00:25:13,612 --> 00:25:15,420
vacations, are visible.

786
00:25:15,600 --> 00:25:16,703
Wealth isn't.

787
00:25:16,883 --> 00:25:19,057
You can't see someone's brokerage account from

788
00:25:19,057 --> 00:25:19,577
the street.

789
00:25:19,757 --> 00:25:22,110
So families optimize for what can be

790
00:25:22,110 --> 00:25:24,006
seen, and neglect what can't.

791
00:25:24,186 --> 00:25:27,517
Thomas Stanley and William Danko described exactly

792
00:25:27,517 --> 00:25:29,782
this in The Millionaire Next Door.

793
00:25:30,132 --> 00:25:32,349
The fourth reason is exhaustion.

794
00:25:32,529 --> 00:25:34,840
Middle class life is busy:

795
00:25:34,840 --> 00:25:36,796
work, kids, commuting,

796
00:25:36,796 --> 00:25:38,485
caring for parents.

797
00:25:38,665 --> 00:25:39,979
When you're exhausted,

798
00:25:39,979 --> 00:25:42,189
the easiest decision is to keep doing

799
00:25:42,189 --> 00:25:43,623
what you've always done.

800
00:25:43,803 --> 00:25:46,101
That's why automation matters so much.

801
00:25:46,281 --> 00:25:48,368
It lets good decisions happen even when

802
00:25:48,368 --> 00:25:49,973
you're too tired to make them.

803
00:25:50,324 --> 00:25:52,441
And the fifth reason is hope that

804
00:25:52,441 --> 00:25:54,109
something big will fix it.

805
00:25:54,289 --> 00:25:56,459
A promotion, an inheritance,

806
00:25:56,459 --> 00:25:57,699
a winning stock,

807
00:25:57,699 --> 00:26:00,024
a house that doubles in value.

808
00:26:00,204 --> 00:26:02,398
People wait for the big break instead

809
00:26:02,398 --> 00:26:04,058
of making small changes now.

810
00:26:04,238 --> 00:26:05,714
But for most families,

811
00:26:05,714 --> 00:26:08,666
wealth comes from small changes repeated for

812
00:26:08,666 --> 00:26:10,879
decades, not from one big moment.

813
00:26:11,230 --> 00:26:12,170
Angle three.

814
00:26:12,350 --> 00:26:13,035
The playbook.

815
00:26:13,215 --> 00:26:15,900
Here's how middle class families break through

816
00:26:15,900 --> 00:26:17,418
the ceiling, in six moves.

817
00:26:17,768 --> 00:26:18,522
Move one.

818
00:26:18,702 --> 00:26:19,875
Know your numbers.

819
00:26:20,055 --> 00:26:22,589
Calculate your net worth with and without

820
00:26:22,589 --> 00:26:24,629
your home, and your savings rate:

821
00:26:24,629 --> 00:26:26,731
the share of your income that goes

822
00:26:26,731 --> 00:26:29,389
to savings, investing and paying down debt.

823
00:26:29,569 --> 00:26:32,031
Most families have never calculated either.

824
00:26:32,211 --> 00:26:34,137
You can't escape a trap you can't

825
00:26:34,137 --> 00:26:34,370
see.

826
00:26:34,720 --> 00:26:35,765
Move two.

827
00:26:35,945 --> 00:26:37,373
Free up the big money.

828
00:26:37,553 --> 00:26:40,675
Housing, cars and child care are usually

829
00:26:40,675 --> 00:26:42,080
the biggest costs.

830
00:26:42,260 --> 00:26:43,956
You don't need to slash all three.

831
00:26:44,136 --> 00:26:45,249
But one big change,

832
00:26:45,249 --> 00:26:47,536
like keeping your cars for years longer

833
00:26:47,536 --> 00:26:49,118
or not upgrading your home,

834
00:26:49,118 --> 00:26:51,287
can free hundreds of dollars a month.

835
00:26:51,467 --> 00:26:52,641
Small savings help,

836
00:26:52,641 --> 00:26:54,741
but the big three move the needle.

837
00:26:55,092 --> 00:26:56,032
Move three.

838
00:26:56,212 --> 00:26:58,589
Automate your way to fifteen percent or

839
00:26:58,589 --> 00:26:58,894
more.

840
00:26:59,074 --> 00:27:01,963
Retirement contributions through payroll,

841
00:27:01,963 --> 00:27:04,429
automatic transfers to an IRA and a

842
00:27:04,429 --> 00:27:07,952
brokerage account, and an automatic increase every

843
00:27:07,952 --> 00:27:08,304
year.

844
00:27:08,484 --> 00:27:10,308
The goal is a system that builds

845
00:27:10,308 --> 00:27:12,931
wealth whether or not you're paying attention.

846
00:27:13,281 --> 00:27:14,059
Move four.

847
00:27:14,239 --> 00:27:15,353
Become an owner.

848
00:27:15,533 --> 00:27:18,089
Invest consistently in low cost,

849
00:27:18,089 --> 00:27:19,527
diversified funds.

850
00:27:19,707 --> 00:27:22,255
Consider other ownership over time,

851
00:27:22,255 --> 00:27:24,949
like a small business or real estate,

852
00:27:24,949 --> 00:27:27,497
only once your foundation is solid.

853
00:27:27,677 --> 00:27:29,460
The more of your income that comes

854
00:27:29,460 --> 00:27:30,404
from what you own,

855
00:27:30,404 --> 00:27:32,082
the less you depend on your next

856
00:27:32,082 --> 00:27:32,553
paycheck.

857
00:27:32,904 --> 00:27:33,774
Move five.

858
00:27:33,954 --> 00:27:35,673
Grow your income on purpose.

859
00:27:35,853 --> 00:27:37,005
Ask for raises,

860
00:27:37,005 --> 00:27:39,463
switch jobs when it makes sense,

861
00:27:39,463 --> 00:27:42,459
build skills, and consider side income.

862
00:27:42,639 --> 00:27:44,524
Then send at least half of every

863
00:27:44,524 --> 00:27:45,820
increase to investing.

864
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:47,347
And move six.

865
00:27:47,527 --> 00:27:48,212
Protect it.

866
00:27:48,392 --> 00:27:51,175
Emergency fund, disability insurance,

867
00:27:51,175 --> 00:27:52,680
term life if needed,

868
00:27:52,680 --> 00:27:54,937
a will, and a written plan for

869
00:27:54,937 --> 00:27:56,066
market crashes.

870
00:27:56,416 --> 00:27:58,270
None of these moves require you to

871
00:27:58,270 --> 00:27:59,307
stop enjoying life.

872
00:27:59,487 --> 00:28:01,746
They require you to decide what matters

873
00:28:01,746 --> 00:28:04,180
most, and to stop letting monthly payments

874
00:28:04,180 --> 00:28:05,744
make that decision for you.

875
00:28:05,924 --> 00:28:08,462
Families who do this often find they're

876
00:28:08,462 --> 00:28:10,023
happier, not less happy,

877
00:28:10,023 --> 00:28:12,820
because money stops being a constant source

878
00:28:12,820 --> 00:28:13,471
of stress.

879
00:28:13,821 --> 00:28:15,977
Let me answer the questions people ask

880
00:28:15,977 --> 00:28:17,849
most about the middle class trap.

881
00:28:18,200 --> 00:28:18,943
Question one.

882
00:28:19,123 --> 00:28:20,818
Is buying a house a mistake?

883
00:28:20,998 --> 00:28:21,230
No.

884
00:28:21,410 --> 00:28:23,188
Owning a home can be a good

885
00:28:23,188 --> 00:28:25,427
way to build wealth and stability.

886
00:28:25,607 --> 00:28:27,619
The trap is buying too much house,

887
00:28:27,619 --> 00:28:29,571
or letting the house be your only

888
00:28:29,571 --> 00:28:29,926
asset.

889
00:28:30,106 --> 00:28:31,000
Question two.

890
00:28:31,180 --> 00:28:33,177
Should we stop saving for college?

891
00:28:33,357 --> 00:28:34,437
Not necessarily.

892
00:28:34,617 --> 00:28:36,718
Just make sure your own retirement is

893
00:28:36,718 --> 00:28:37,570
on track first,

894
00:28:37,570 --> 00:28:39,558
and be realistic about how much you

895
00:28:39,558 --> 00:28:40,410
can contribute.

896
00:28:40,760 --> 00:28:41,839
Question three.

897
00:28:42,019 --> 00:28:43,796
We're in our forties and stuck.

898
00:28:43,976 --> 00:28:45,079
Is it too late?

899
00:28:45,259 --> 00:28:45,468
No.

900
00:28:45,648 --> 00:28:47,927
Your forties and fifties are often your

901
00:28:47,927 --> 00:28:49,038
peak earning years.

902
00:28:49,218 --> 00:28:51,355
Freeing up a thousand dollars a month

903
00:28:51,355 --> 00:28:53,319
and investing it for twenty years,

904
00:28:53,319 --> 00:28:55,167
at a seven percent illustration,

905
00:28:55,167 --> 00:28:57,535
could grow to roughly five hundred twenty

906
00:28:57,535 --> 00:28:58,517
thousand dollars.

907
00:28:58,697 --> 00:29:00,416
That alone can break the ceiling.

908
00:29:00,766 --> 00:29:01,694
Question four.

909
00:29:01,874 --> 00:29:03,416
How do we talk to our kids

910
00:29:03,416 --> 00:29:04,069
about this?

911
00:29:04,249 --> 00:29:06,559
Be honest in an age appropriate way.

912
00:29:06,739 --> 00:29:09,085
Explain that you're choosing to save for

913
00:29:09,085 --> 00:29:10,258
the family's future,

914
00:29:10,258 --> 00:29:12,370
and involve them in small decisions.

915
00:29:12,550 --> 00:29:14,784
Kids who learn early that money is

916
00:29:14,784 --> 00:29:16,427
a tool, not a scoreboard,

917
00:29:16,427 --> 00:29:18,596
are more likely to avoid the trap

918
00:29:18,596 --> 00:29:19,319
themselves.

919
00:29:19,669 --> 00:29:20,690
Question five.

920
00:29:20,870 --> 00:29:22,728
What if our neighbors judge us for

921
00:29:22,728 --> 00:29:23,657
driving old cars?

922
00:29:23,837 --> 00:29:25,598
Remember, you see their car,

923
00:29:25,598 --> 00:29:26,542
not their debt.

924
00:29:26,722 --> 00:29:28,460
Many of the people who look the

925
00:29:28,460 --> 00:29:30,367
wealthiest are the most stretched.

926
00:29:30,547 --> 00:29:31,849
And in twenty years,

927
00:29:31,849 --> 00:29:34,193
nobody will remember what you drove.

928
00:29:34,373 --> 00:29:36,025
They'll notice that you're relaxed,

929
00:29:36,025 --> 00:29:37,252
and they're still working.

930
00:29:37,602 --> 00:29:38,658
Question seven.

931
00:29:38,838 --> 00:29:40,396
We earn a good income but feel

932
00:29:40,396 --> 00:29:40,707
broke.

933
00:29:40,887 --> 00:29:41,932
Where do we start?

934
00:29:42,112 --> 00:29:44,458
Start with a one month spending review.

935
00:29:44,638 --> 00:29:47,217
Write down every fixed payment:

936
00:29:47,217 --> 00:29:48,631
mortgage or rent,

937
00:29:48,631 --> 00:29:51,211
cars, insurance, subscriptions,

938
00:29:51,211 --> 00:29:52,625
child care, debt.

939
00:29:52,805 --> 00:29:55,026
For most families who feel broke on

940
00:29:55,026 --> 00:29:55,915
a good income,

941
00:29:55,915 --> 00:29:57,628
the answer is in that list,

942
00:29:57,628 --> 00:29:59,342
not in coffee or groceries.

943
00:29:59,522 --> 00:30:02,149
Pick the biggest payment you can realistically

944
00:30:02,149 --> 00:30:03,519
change in the next year,

945
00:30:03,519 --> 00:30:05,176
and make a plan to change it.

946
00:30:05,526 --> 00:30:06,687
Question six.

947
00:30:06,867 --> 00:30:08,858
Should we use our home equity to

948
00:30:08,858 --> 00:30:09,293
invest?

949
00:30:09,473 --> 00:30:11,836
Borrowing against your home to invest adds

950
00:30:11,836 --> 00:30:13,918
risk, because you could lose money in

951
00:30:13,918 --> 00:30:15,831
the market and still owe the debt,

952
00:30:15,831 --> 00:30:17,519
with your house as collateral.

953
00:30:17,699 --> 00:30:18,792
For most families,

954
00:30:18,792 --> 00:30:21,465
it's safer to build investments from monthly

955
00:30:21,465 --> 00:30:23,590
cash flow, and let home equity grow

956
00:30:23,590 --> 00:30:24,258
on its own.

957
00:30:24,609 --> 00:30:26,757
Let's recap the fifteen traps.

958
00:30:26,937 --> 00:30:29,050
The house is the only asset.

959
00:30:29,230 --> 00:30:30,298
Too much house.

960
00:30:30,478 --> 00:30:31,859
Car payments forever.

961
00:30:32,039 --> 00:30:33,978
Lifestyle that matches income.

962
00:30:34,158 --> 00:30:35,772
Saving without investing.

963
00:30:35,952 --> 00:30:37,926
Investing just enough for the match.

964
00:30:38,106 --> 00:30:39,510
Income that stalls.

965
00:30:39,690 --> 00:30:41,620
And funding the kids before your own

966
00:30:41,620 --> 00:30:42,210
retirement.

967
00:30:42,560 --> 00:30:44,278
Revolving credit card debt.

968
00:30:44,458 --> 00:30:45,607
Fees that compound.

969
00:30:45,787 --> 00:30:47,970
Ignoring tax advantages.

970
00:30:48,150 --> 00:30:49,102
No protection.

971
00:30:49,282 --> 00:30:50,606
Panic in the crash.

972
00:30:50,786 --> 00:30:52,156
Only earning, never owning.

973
00:30:52,336 --> 00:30:54,850
And the most expensive trap of all:

974
00:30:54,850 --> 00:30:56,144
waiting for later.

975
00:30:56,494 --> 00:30:57,666
And the three angles.

976
00:30:57,846 --> 00:31:00,792
The numbers: middle class wealth is mostly

977
00:31:00,792 --> 00:31:04,018
home equity, and the median retirement account

978
00:31:04,018 --> 00:31:04,650
is small.

979
00:31:04,830 --> 00:31:06,799
The psychology: social norms,

980
00:31:06,799 --> 00:31:08,565
payments that hide prices,

981
00:31:08,565 --> 00:31:10,874
and success you can see instead of

982
00:31:10,874 --> 00:31:12,028
wealth you can't.

983
00:31:12,208 --> 00:31:13,349
And the playbook:

984
00:31:13,349 --> 00:31:14,558
know your numbers,

985
00:31:14,558 --> 00:31:16,035
free up the big money,

986
00:31:16,035 --> 00:31:17,714
automate fifteen percent,

987
00:31:17,714 --> 00:31:18,788
become an owner,

988
00:31:18,788 --> 00:31:19,930
grow your income,

989
00:31:19,930 --> 00:31:21,206
and protect it all.

990
00:31:21,556 --> 00:31:23,950
The middle class trap isn't a conspiracy,

991
00:31:23,950 --> 00:31:25,352
and it isn't your fault.

992
00:31:25,532 --> 00:31:27,635
It's the natural result of a culture

993
00:31:27,635 --> 00:31:29,270
that sells monthly payments,

994
00:31:29,270 --> 00:31:31,665
a housing market that rewards stretching,

995
00:31:31,665 --> 00:31:33,943
and a retirement system that depends on

996
00:31:33,943 --> 00:31:35,052
you doing the work.

997
00:31:35,232 --> 00:31:36,450
But once you see it,

998
00:31:36,450 --> 00:31:37,850
you can step out of it,

999
00:31:37,850 --> 00:31:39,249
one decision at a time.

1000
00:31:39,429 --> 00:31:40,265
Keep the car.

1001
00:31:40,445 --> 00:31:41,606
Invest the raise.

1002
00:31:41,786 --> 00:31:43,447
Choose the smaller house.

1003
00:31:43,627 --> 00:31:45,600
Stay invested in the crash.

1004
00:31:45,780 --> 00:31:47,661
Those choices look small.

1005
00:31:47,841 --> 00:31:48,908
Over twenty years,

1006
00:31:48,908 --> 00:31:51,517
they're the difference between a ceiling and

1007
00:31:51,517 --> 00:31:52,288
a launch pad.

1008
00:31:52,638 --> 00:31:54,089
So here's your homework.

1009
00:31:54,269 --> 00:31:56,353
Pick the one trap from this list

1010
00:31:56,353 --> 00:31:58,437
that costs your family the most.

1011
00:31:58,617 --> 00:31:59,244
Just one.

1012
00:31:59,424 --> 00:32:01,049
And make one change this month.

1013
00:32:01,229 --> 00:32:02,453
Then, next month,

1014
00:32:02,453 --> 00:32:03,749
pick the next one.

1015
00:32:03,929 --> 00:32:06,066
Fifteen traps sounds like a lot,

1016
00:32:06,066 --> 00:32:08,337
but most families only need to fix

1017
00:32:08,337 --> 00:32:10,407
three or four to break through.

1018
00:32:10,587 --> 00:32:12,259
If you want the full step by

1019
00:32:12,259 --> 00:32:14,111
step plan, it's in my playbook,

1020
00:32:14,111 --> 00:32:15,544
link in the description.

1021
00:32:15,724 --> 00:32:16,885
I'm Ryan Rover.

1022
00:32:17,065 --> 00:32:18,284
See you in the next one.
